CLINE APC represents buyers and lessees of faulty Lincoln vehicles - and fights for fair compensation under California lemon law.

## Lincoln Models with the Most Recalls

[Lincoln Motor Company](https://www.lincoln.com/) is the luxury division of Ford. Founded in 1917, the brand has built a strong reputation for both quality and style. Lincoln is continuously growing its catalog as it expands its global reach - but ongoing success doesn’t always equal perfection. Even some of Lincoln’s most renowned models have been subjected to recalls. Here are the top five with the most mechanical defects.

## Lincoln Town Car

The Lincoln Town Car is the brand’s famous full-size sedan - produced from 1980-2011. Through three generations, over a million units were sold. Despite the vehicle’s success on the global market, it holds the record for most recalls under the Lincoln brand with 113. These recalls are for issues including:

- Seatbelt malfunctions

- Steering problems

- Tire issues

- Speed control defects

## Lincoln Continental

The Continental is one of Lincoln’s oldest models, first produced in 1939 and discontinued in 2020. Through 10 generations, the Continental amassed 63 NHTSA recalls for a range of problems. Some of the most notable include:

- Service brake failures

- Structural defects

- Transmission malfunctions

- Exterior lighting issues

## Lincoln MKZ

The MKZ is a full-size sedan known for innovations in the gas/hybrid market. The model was in production from 2005-2020, earning favorable reviews from critics and consumers. Most of the vehicle’s recalls came in the 2010s - with 62 on record for problems including:

- Airbag defects

- Steering issues

- Seatbelt malfunctions

- Service brake failures

## Lincoln Navigator

The Navigator is a renowned full-size luxury SUV, launched in 1998. The model is a favorite in North America and is considered the equivalent of the Ford Expedition. The Navigator was named [Truck of the Year in 2018](https://northamericancaroftheyear.org/2018-nactoy-winners-announced/) by the American International Auto Show. Throughout the vehicle’s commercial and critical success, it has been subjected to 37 major recalls, for issues including:

- Speed control problems

- Automatic transmission malfunctions

- Visibility issues

- Fuel system defects

## Lincoln MKX

The MKX - a mid-size crossover SUV - has been in production since 2006 and has sold more units than any other Lincoln since 2016. The MKX is generally considered one of the best luxury SUVs on the market, but it’s no stranger to defects. The model has been recalled 20 times for problems related to:

- Airbag malfunctions

- Faulty electrical systems

- Fuel system issues

## Does Your Vehicle Qualify for Lincoln Lemon Law in California?

Purchasing or leasing a defective Lincoln is a stressful experience. Simply finding a defect is not always enough to warrant a lemon law claim. To be eligible for a Lincoln lemon law buyback in California, your vehicle must meet the following qualifications:

- The Lincoln’s warranty was valid when the defect(s) was reported to the automaker; and

- The vehicle has one or more substantial defect(s) that compromises the safety, functionality, or value of the vehicle; and

- Lincoln’s certified repair technicians have made a reasonable number of repair attempts (usually at least two) to repair the defect(s); or

- The Lincoln vehicle has been out of service for repairs for 30 or more cumulative days; and

- The defect was not due to driver neglect or abuse.

To meet these qualifications and file a Lincoln lemon law claim, you need to have all the [appropriate documentation](/blog/california-lemon-law-claim-documents) in order. This includes repair orders, invoices, receipts, communication with the manufacturer, and all other records.

## Lincoln Lemon Law FAQs

## Which vehicles are eligible for a Lincoln lemon law buyback?

Any Lincoln vehicle sold with a manufacturer or dealership warranty may be eligible for benefits under the California lemon law. You must be able to prove the defect or nonconformity was reported to Lincoln’s representatives or the dealership within the terms of the warranty.

## What types of compensation may I earn in the Lincoln buyback program?

Once your Lincoln vehicle meets the qualifications of [California lemon law](/info/a-comprehensive-guide-to-california-lemon-law), you may pursue the following compensation:

- The total cost of the Lincoln vehicle (minus a mileage offset).

- Down payment

- Monthly payments made

- The remaining balance of the loan

- All official costs paid

- Sales tax

- Registration

- All incidental costs stemming from the lemon

- Towing fees

- Uber/Lyft/Cab fares

- Hotel stays

- Meals

- Etc.

- All attorney fees and court costs

## May I still seek a Lincoln lemon law buyback if the warranty is expired?

You may only file a California lemon law claim if the defect in question was reported to the dealer or manufacturer within the terms of the warranty the vehicle was sold with. If there are records proving the defect was reported while the warranty was in effect, it doesn’t matter if the warranty expires during the repair process.

## Is there a time limit to file a Lincoln lemon law claim?

Yes. There is a four-year statute of limitations to file for the Lincoln buyback program in California. However, we strongly recommend filing a claim as soon as the vehicle meets the California lemon law qualifications. The longer you wait, the weaker your case becomes. For example, waiting two years to file a claim indicates the defect is not “substantial” enough to warrant a Lincoln [lemon law buyback](/blog/lemon-law-buybacks-what-to-expect) - making your path to fair compensation more difficult.

Hire a lemon law lawyer in California as soon as your vehicle is experiencing recurring defects.

## Are used vehicles eligible for the Lincoln buyback program?

Yes. Lincoln lemon law extends to used vehicles, assuming they are sold with a valid warranty. Used car dealerships in California typically sell vehicles with a warranty good for 30 days or before 1,000 miles accrue on the odometer. If your vehicle meets the qualifications of California lemon law within the terms of the dealer warranty, it may be processed in the same way as a new vehicle.

## What if I bought my Lincoln “as is”?

Unfortunately, you probably don’t have any warranty protection under California lemon law. “As is” agreements mean the consumer takes full responsibility for any defects or nonconformities once the vehicle is driven off the lot. Dealerships are required to specify if there is a warranty in place or if the vehicle is being sold “as is” on the buyer’s guide - meaning there is [no warranty](/blog/used-car-no-warranty-lemon-law-california).

## What if there is no dealer warranty or “as is” agreement listed on the buyer’s guide?

If there is no specification of a dealer warranty or “as is” agreement, there may be an [implied warranty of merchantability](https://www.justia.com/trials-litigation/docs/caci/1200/1231/) in place. This is an unspoken warranty that the vehicle is fit for its intended purpose - and is good for 60 days up to a year.

Implied warranties are not always black and white. Speak with an experienced lemon law lawyer in California to learn more about your options.

## How much does a Lincoln lemon law lawyer cost?

Under California lemon law, the manufacturer is required to pay for the attorney and court costs as part of the Lincoln buyback program. For this reason, most lemon law attorneys work on a [contingency fee agreement](https://www.calbar.ca.gov/Public/Free-Legal-Information/Working-with-an-Attorney/What-to-Expect-Regarding-Fees-and-Billing).Manufacturers always pay our attorney fees under California Lemon Law. We do not take a percentage of your buyback. In other words, it shouldn’t cost you anything to hire an attorney for a Lincoln lemon law buyback.

